WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.362
Python database access often sits behind a framework or ORM.

2
00:00:03.362 --> 00:00:17.007
Django centralizes many database settings, SQLAlchemy-style engines
collect driver and pool configuration, and Flask or FastAPI applications
commonly assemble a data layer with dependency injection, sessions, or
request lifecycle hooks.

3
00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:21.556
Those abstractions are helpful, but they do not guarantee secure
configuration.

4
00:00:21.556 --> 00:00:30.521
Reviewers should still confirm how secrets are loaded, whether TLS
expectations are enforced, which account is used, and how connections are
opened and closed.

5
00:00:30.521 --> 00:00:35.992
ORMs can make common workflows safer and more maintainable, yet they also
provide escape hatches.

6
00:00:35.992 --> 00:00:44.364
Raw SQL, dynamic filters, custom connection options, and multiple engine
definitions may bypass the assumptions used in the rest of the
application.

7
00:00:44.364 --> 00:00:54.516
A secure connection standard should apply consistently whether code uses a
high-level model, a migration tool, an async driver, a reporting query, or
a lower-level database cursor.

8
00:00:54.516 --> 00:01:00.647
Migrations need special handling because they often require privileges
normal runtime code should not have.

9
00:01:00.647 --> 00:01:08.689
Schema changes, index creation, data backfills, and administrative
maintenance can require broader authority and longer execution windows.

10
00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:12.908
That does not mean the web application should run forever with those
powers.

11
00:01:12.908 --> 00:01:22.268
Migration credentials, deployment jobs, approval processes, and audit logs
should make privileged database changes intentional, time-bounded, and
attributable.

12
00:01:22.268 --> 00:01:28.663
Background jobs, scheduled tasks, notebooks, and data scripts frequently
become exceptions to secure patterns.

13
00:01:28.663 --> 00:01:36.046
They may be written quickly, run with copied connection strings, skip TLS
options, log full errors, or use production data casually.

14
00:01:36.046 --> 00:01:40.726
Treat every code path that connects to the database as part of the
security boundary.

15
00:01:40.726 --> 00:01:50.680
The same expectations for secret handling, environment separation, least
privilege, logging hygiene, and safe failure behavior should follow the
data wherever it is accessed.
